::: {.doc-subject}

Detect communities using an optimized Girvan-Newman algorithm.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L38775-L38947){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.cmty_girvan_newman(
    attr_name=None,
    return_dict=False,
    most_valuable_edge=None,
    n_communities=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Girvan-Newman communities](../assets/graphs/cmty-girvan-newman.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Two dense clusters, a triangle A, B, C and a four-node cluster D, E, F, G, joined by the bridge C to D, drawn dashed because it has the highest betweenness and is cut first. It shares cmty_edge_btwns()'s implementation, so the result is identical: community 0 is A, B, C (accent) and community 1 is D, E, F, G (teal)." width="100%"}

Iteratively removes the edge with highest betweenness centrality to reveal hierarchical
community structure, returning the partition that maximizes Newman-Girvan modularity, or
another level of the hierarchy with `n_communities=`. In `play()`, each split is a frame of
its own. Betweenness is recomputed only for the component that changed, with parallel BFS.
`cmty_edge_btwns()` runs the same implementation and returns the same partition; this
method also offers weighted betweenness via `most_valuable_edge="weight"`.

When several edges tie for the highest betweenness, the one with the smallest endpoint
IDs is removed first, so results are deterministic. (Other tools may break ties
differently and so end up with a different, equally valid dendrogram.) Communities are
numbered by their smallest node ID.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Column name to store community assignments in the node DataFrame. If `None`
    (default), automatically uses `cmty_girvan_newman`. Overwrites column if it
    already exists.

<code>[return_dict]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, returns dictionary instead of self. If `False` (default), stores in
    NDF and returns self for chaining.

<code>[most_valuable_edge]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Strategy for selecting which edge to remove. If `"weight"`, uses edge weight in
    the betweenness calculation (Dijkstra instead of BFS). If `None` (default), treats
    the graph as unweighted.

<code>[n_communities]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Return the level of the hierarchy with this many communities, instead of the one
    with the highest modularity. Each removal that disconnects the graph adds one
    community, so any number from the graph's count of connected components up to its
    number of nodes is available. If `None` (default), returns the partition with the
    highest modularity.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Union[dict[int, int], Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `return_dict=` is `True`: dictionary mapping node IDs to community IDs
    (0-indexed, contiguous). If `return_dict=` is `False` (default): returns self for
    method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If graph is directed (requires undirected graph), or if no level of the hierarchy
    has `n_communities=` communities.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~cmty_edge_btwns`) 

:   The same algorithm, returning the partition only.

[](`~edge_betweenness_centrality`) 

:   The edge scores that decide which edge goes next.

[](`~modularity`) 

:   The score that picks the level of the hierarchy.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The bridge between these two triangles has the highest betweenness, so it is the first
edge removed. That splits the graph into the two triangles, the partition with the highest
modularity:

::: {#522b3084 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges([
    ("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A"),
    ("C", "D"),
    ("D", "E"), ("E", "F"), ("F", "D"),
])

(
    graph.cmty_girvan_newman()
    .get_node_df()
    .select("label", "cmty_girvan_newman")
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
